 have some  subsequent system is that a candidate  can end up winning without being  anybody's first choice so you could have  a situation in normal rank choice voting  where candidate a ends up with twelve  percent of the vote in the first  around but somebody else had six so  candidate a makes it to the second round  and in the second round candidate a ends  up with 32 percent of the vote and  they've still not received the plurality  but it's enough to go to the next round  and therefore you can end up with the  candidate winning who was it anybody's  actual first choice on the basis of  being a plurality of voters second third  or fourth choice or something like that  that does seem like a problem with rank  choice voting doesn't it yeah I like  this thought experiment I think it's  just getting so overly complex right I  mean ranked choice voting voting is  already tough enough to explain to some  people yes and it's though it's hard to  push for dorset anything else yeah and  even more complex and the other problem  with the Condorcet type rules is that  number one it's very logistical ii  difficult to count in large elections  and also you need people to agree as to  what the subsequent method would be for  ranking so to answer the question I  don't actually know specifically what  type of method I would prefer to normal  ranked choice but I recognize that there  is a problem where it doesn't seem  democratic where a candidate that isn't  anybody's first choice ends up being the  winning candidate that seems  fundamentally wrong to me and I don't  actually have the answer yeah and the  one-person one-vote thing is a problem  with the ranked choice voting to Inc -  but you also see that with the electoral  college right  yeah that's why ideally we would get rid  of that and replace it with the popular  vote maybe the states could have the  option to put in ranked choice voting as  at the individual level yeah and we're  already seeing by the way that states  making those efforts including Maine are  meeting with pretty significant  opposition when it actually comes to  enacted so I'm actually it's just an  open question I'm comfortable telling  you that I'm not totally sure I've  identified not that I've come up with it  but I've read quite a bit about the  problems with normal ranked choice  voting but I've not yet sort of taken a  strong position as to which system I  would prefer
 

